### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to compute the difference between two dates using the java.time API. This tests ChronoUnit usage, signed vs absolute differences, and the distinction between ChronoUnit and Period.

### How do you calculate the difference in days?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;
import java.util.*;

public class DateDifference {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate rajeshJoined = LocalDate.of(2015, 3, 1);
        LocalDate priyaJoined = LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 15);

        long daysBetween = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
            rajeshJoined, priyaJoined);

        System.out.println("Days between: " + daysBetween);
        System.out.println("Rajesh joined " + daysBetween
            + " days before Priya");
    }
}
```

```text
Days between: 2298
Rajesh joined 2298 days before Priya
```

### How is ChronoUnit different from Period?

| Feature | ChronoUnit.DAYS.between | Period.between |
|---|---|---|
| Returns | long (flat total days) | Period (years + months + days) |
| Example | 2298 | 6 years, 3 months, 14 days |
| Use case | Total days for calculations | Human-readable breakdown |
| Other units | MONTHS, YEARS, WEEKS, HOURS | Only years/months/days |

```java
import java.time.Period;
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;

LocalDate start = LocalDate.of(2015, 3, 1);
LocalDate end = LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 15);

// ChronoUnit: flat total
long totalDays = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(start, end);    // 2298
long totalMonths = ChronoUnit.MONTHS.between(start, end); // 75
long totalYears = ChronoUnit.YEARS.between(start, end);   // 6

// Period: breakdown
Period period = Period.between(start, end);
System.out.println(period.getYears() + "y "
    + period.getMonths() + "m "
    + period.getDays() + "d");
// 6y 3m 14d
```

### How do you use this with streams for pairwise comparison?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class TenureComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        // Compute tenure in days for each employee
        employees.stream()
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getJoinDate))
            .forEach(e -> {
                long tenure = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
                    e.getJoinDate(), java.time.LocalDate.now());
                System.out.printf("%s: %d days (%.1f years)%n",
                    e.getName(), tenure, tenure / 365.25);
            });
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: reversing the argument order and getting a negative result.

```java
// Returns negative when start is after end
long negative = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
    LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 15),
    LocalDate.of(2015, 3, 1));  // -2298

// Use Math.abs for absolute difference
long absolute = Math.abs(ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
    LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 15),
    LocalDate.of(2015, 3, 1)));  // 2298
```

Mistake 2: using Period.getDays() expecting total days.

```java
// WRONG: Period.getDays() returns only the days component, not total
Period p = Period.between(
    LocalDate.of(2015, 3, 1),
    LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 15));
int wrong = p.getDays();  // 14 (just the days part, not 2298!)

// CORRECT: use ChronoUnit for total
long correct = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
    LocalDate.of(2015, 3, 1),
    LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 15));  // 2298
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find the pair of employees with the largest joining date gap?" Compare all pairs using nested streams or a sorted list.
- "How do you compute average tenure?" Map each employee to tenure days, then use averagingLong.
- "How do you handle time zones?" Use ZonedDateTime and ChronoUnit.DAYS.between still works.
